{:edb/db-before-t 1188 :edb/db-after-t 1189 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534501 50 #inst "2026-10-07T18:14:57.927Z" 13194139534501 true] [17592186175825 1020 #edb/ref 17592186292138 13194139534501 true] [17592186175827 1020 #edb/ref 17592186292124 13194139534501 true] [17592186175827 1020 #edb/ref 17592186292131 13194139534501 true] [17592186175827 1020 #edb/ref 17592186292145 13194139534501 true] [17592186176415 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534501 true] [17592186176415 1100 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,{x}^{2}+1, & x<1 \\\\ -x+2, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $[0,2]$\n- $(1,2)$\n- $[-2,2]$" 13194139534501 true] [17592186176415 1100 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n$f(x) = {x^{2} + 1,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 1 \\\\ -x + 2,\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge 1$.\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n1. $[0, 2]$\n2. $(1, 2)$\n3. $[- 2, 2]$" 13194139534501 false] [17592186176415 1145 "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- Consider $[0,2].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}({x}^{2}+1)=(1)^{2}+1=2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(-x+2)=-1+2=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=1$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[0,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- The interval $(1,2)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Finally, consider $[-2,2].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=1,$ which is belongs to the interval $[-2,2].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-2,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"None\"." 13194139534501 true] [17592186292124 1017 #edb/ref 17592186292125 13194139534501 true] [17592186292124 1089 "q-129216" 13194139534501 true] [17592186292124 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534501 true] [17592186292124 1100 "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x?$    \n\n- $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$\n- $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$\n- $[-\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}]$" 13194139534501 true] [17592186292124 1113 #uuid "488dc15f-8e24-5a7f-9292-8ed02d5ff2e8" 13194139534501 true] [17592186292124 1145 "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$  is closed, but the function is not defined at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[-\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[-\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139534501 true] [17592186292125 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292126 13194139534501 true] [17592186292125 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292127 13194139534501 true] [17592186292125 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292128 13194139534501 true] [17592186292125 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292129 13194139534501 true] [17592186292125 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292130 13194139534501 true] [17592186292125 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534501 true] [17592186292125 1041 "selection" 13194139534501 true] [17592186292125 1104 #edb/ref 17592186292129 13194139534501 true] [17592186292125 1110 #uuid "dd050d78-7eef-5cd7-bdff-b