uous on $[1,2].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval $(0,2)$  is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139534501 true] [17592186292132 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292133 13194139534501 true] [17592186292132 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292134 13194139534501 true] [17592186292132 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292135 13194139534501 true] [17592186292132 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292136 13194139534501 true] [17592186292132 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292137 13194139534501 true] [17592186292132 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534501 true] [17592186292132 1041 "selection" 13194139534501 true] [17592186292132 1104 #edb/ref 17592186292137 13194139534501 true] [17592186292132 1110 #uuid "e509ccf0-c772-59a4-8963-fd5e37f39ccb" 13194139534501 true] [17592186292133 1034 "I only" 13194139534501 true] [17592186292133 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534501 true] [17592186292133 1111 #uuid "218219ca-b14d-5e0d-a5b9-b4a0d6236399" 13194139534501 true] [17592186292134 1034 "III only" 13194139534501 true] [17592186292134 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534501 true] [17592186292134 1111 #uuid "98ff2d2c-b476-5a89-b7e6-ca3d0cb5c10c" 13194139534501 true] [17592186292135 1034 "None" 13194139534501 true] [17592186292135 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534501 true] [17592186292135 1111 #uuid "59fd3680-2861-5f78-b393-bc502864e00d" 13194139534501 true] [17592186292136 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534501 true] [17592186292136 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534501 true] [17592186292136 1111 #uuid "33091967-14d3-5fc7-be5a-c989a2861e7c" 13194139534501 true] [17592186292137 1034 "II only" 13194139534501 true] [17592186292137 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534501 true] [17592186292137 1111 #uuid "b26d08b8-252f-56de-b2cd-432b01ba49e5" 13194139534501 true] [17592186292138 1017 #edb/ref 17592186292139 13194139534501 true] [17592186292138 1089 "q-131180" 13194139534501 true] [17592186292138 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534501 true] [17592186292138 1100 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-1, & x<-1 \\\\ {x}^{3}, & -1\\le x<1 \\\\ 1, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $(-1,1)$\n- $[-2,0]$\n- $[0,2]$" 13194139534501 true] [17592186292138 1113 #uuid "39192d32-1d30-5a83-a103-b59ae37c1939" 13194139534501 true] [17592186292138 1145 "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- The interval $(-1,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Now, consider $[-2,0].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}(-1)=-1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}({x}^{3})=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=-1$ are the same. Hence, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[-2,0],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, consider $[0,2].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}({x}^{3})=1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(1)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=1$ are the same. Hence, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[0,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139534501 true] [17592186292139 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292140 13194139534501 true] [17592186292139 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292141 13194139534501 true] [17592186292139 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292142 13194139534501 true] [17592186292139 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292143 13194139534501 true] [17592186292139 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292144 13194139534501 true] [17592186292139 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 131941395